INT. PRIVATE LIBRARY OF A COUNTRY HOUSE - NIGHT

Firelight. Leather spines. A storm throwing rain at the leaded windows.

Four people sit in wing chairs around a low table: a decanter, a deck of cards, a bowl of walnuts.

SIR AUGUSTUS FERRIDAY-OKONKWO (70s, silver mustache, velvet jacket) holds a glass of port and a closed fan of cards.

His niece LADY PHILOMENA FERRIDAY (30s, emerald gown, pearl choker) taps a walnut against the table.

Their guest DR. BARNABY CHATTERJEE-WHITLOCK (50s, rumpled tweed, wire glasses) studies his hand.

The butler, MR. SAMUEL OKAFOR-LYNCH (60s, impeccable, immaculately still), stands behind the fireplace.

Thunder rolls. The cards are placed one by one.

SIR AUGUSTUS
Your play, Doctor.

DR. CHATTERJEE-WHITLOCK
Patience, Sir Augustus.

He lays a card. Smiles faintly.

PHILOMENA
You're stalling.

DR. CHATTERJEE-WHITLOCK
I'm thinking.

PHILOMENA
Same thing.

SIR AUGUSTUS
Samuel, more port.

MR. OKAFOR-LYNCH
Of course, Sir Augustus.

He glides forward, fills the glass, glides back. The fire pops.

PHILOMENA
Uncle, the will.

SIR AUGUSTUS
Not tonight.

PHILOMENA
You said tonight.

SIR AUGUSTUS
I said some night.

DR. CHATTERJEE-WHITLOCK
It's a sensitive subject, my dear.

PHILOMENA
It's my inheritance.

SIR AUGUSTUS
It's mine until I'm dead.

Another thunderclap. The lights flicker. The room dips to gold, then to near-black. A glass shatters. Someone gasps.

The lights return.

Sir Augustus is slumped in his chair, one hand clutching the arm, the other still gripping the port. His mustache is wet. His eyes are open. He does not breathe.

Philomenna rises first, knocking the walnut bowl over. Chatterjee-Whitlock is already at the old man's side with two fingers on the throat. Okafor-Lynch has not moved from the fireplace.

PHILOMENA
Uncle?

DR. CHATTERJEE-WHITLOCK
(quietly)
He's gone.

PHILOMENA
That's not possible. He was just talking.

DR. CHATTERJEE-WHITLOCK
It happens quickly.

She looks at the decanter. At the butler. At the doctor. She picks up the port, sniffs it.

PHILOMENA
Bitter almond.

DR. CHATTERJEE-WHITLOCK
Philomena, don't touch--

PHILOMENA
Samuel poured it.

MR. OKAFOR-LYNCH
Madam, I poured what Sir Augustus requested.

PHILOMENA
You poured it twice.

MR. OKAFOR-LYNCH
Once for the glass. Once for his health.

She lunges at him. He doesn't flinch. The doctor catches her arm. Chatterjee-Whitlock holds her back as she strains, trembling, at the butler.

PHILOMENA
You killed him!

MR. OKAFOR-LYNCH
(softly)
Madam, I loved him for forty years.

The storm breaks. Rain hammers the glass. For the first time, Okafor-Lynch's composure cracks. He reaches into his waistcoat and withdraws a folded paper.

MR. OKAFOR-LYNCH (CONT'D)
He wrote this himself, this afternoon. It names the poison. And the man who put it in the decanter.

He hands it to the doctor. The doctor reads. His face drains of color.

DR. CHATTERJEE-WHITLOCK
(whispering)
I didn't--

He steps back. Philomena stares at him. The fire collapses into embers.
